 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to reporting requirements for various classifications of gas leaks by utility corporations.",
 "title": "Relates to reporting requirements for various classifications of gas leaks by utility corporations",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A467",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A467-2017",
     "printNo": "A467",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 267\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 467-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the public service law, in relation to reporting of\n      natural gas leaks by gas corporations\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would amend the Public Service Law to adopt a gas leak clas-\nsification  system  in  statute,  establish new reporting for gas leaks,\nsafety requirements for underground work and direct the  Public  Service\nCommission (the Commission) to commence a proceeding to determine wheth-\ner  gas  corporations  should  conduct  winter surveillance of cast iron\npipes.\n \n  I fully support the intent of this bill to bolster gas safety  and  to\nrequire  gas  corporations  to  monitor and report detected leaks to the\nCommission. However, the bill contains fatal flaws that would require  a\nchange  in  State law any time a corresponding federal standard changes.\nSpecifically, this bill would codify almost  verbatim  the  State's  gas\nleak  classification  regulations.  Under federal law, the Commission is\nresponsible for adopting minimum pipeline safety requirements  in  regu-\nlation and the State's regulations must be consistent with federal stan-\ndards.  Codifying these rules would not only make it more cumbersome for\nthe State to remain consistent with  federal  standards,  but  it  would\njeopardize federal funding provided to the Commission for administration\nof   the  pipeline  safety  program.  For  those  reasons  alone,  I  am\nconstrained to veto the bill.\n \n  However, one key aspect of the bill merits further attention from  the\nCommission.  The  bill would have required gas corporations to regularly\nreport detected leaks to the Commission and make such information avail-\nable to public safety officials. The legislative goal  of  communicating\nthe  presence of leaks to public safety officials is a worthy one, and I\ntherefore direct the Commission to  review  current  reporting  require-\nments,  determine  what  additional  reporting and notification measures\nwould fulfill this legislative objective and thereafter commence a rule-\nmaking process to seek additional stakeholder input and promulgate addi-\ntional reporting measures that improve communication  among  gas  corpo-\nrations, the Commission and relevant public safety officials.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 267,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2018
   }
